  "content": {
    "id": "p40-q75-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "75",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{(1-x)^n}$ and $g(x)=\\dfrac{(1-x)^n}{x}$, where $n>1$. Find the value of $\\alpha$, in terms of $n$, such that $f'(\\alpha)=g'(\\alpha)=0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p40-q75-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\alpha=\\dfrac{1}{1-n}",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p40-q75-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Both quotient-rule derivatives have the factor ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "1+(n-1)x",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " in their numerators.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\dfrac{(1-x)^n+nx(1-x)^{n-1}}{(1-x)^{2n}}\\\\f'(x)&=\\dfrac{1+(n-1)x}{(1-x)^{n+1}},\\\\g'(x)&=\\dfrac{-nx(1-x)^{n-1}-(1-x)^n}{x^2}\\\\g'(x)&=-\\dfrac{(1-x)^{n-1}[1+(n-1)x]}{x^2}.\\end{align*}$$\n\n Both derivatives vanish when $1+(n-1)\\alpha=0$, giving $\\alpha=\\dfrac{1}{1-n}$. Since $n>1$, this value is neither $0$ nor $1$, so both functions are defined there.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
